So what's still worth discussing?

I'd stop adding architecture boxes and attack these 5 failure cases:

Scheduler crashes after launching A1 but before recording it
Task Status Service receives duplicate/out-of-order events
A1 loses heartbeat → A2 starts → A1 comes back
A1 and A2 both report SUCCESS simultaneously
Job Controller crashes halfway through creating 100 tasks

If you can design those correctly, your design is genuinely interview-ready, rather than just having a nice-looking architecture diagram.

And #4 is particularly good: it forces you to explain how exactly one Attempt becomes the logical winner despite races. That's the distributed-systems heart of this design.

