                              Task Status Service receives duplicate/out-of-order events
                                    Yes, but I'd make this slightly stronger than timestamp.

                                    Suppose:

                                    A1:
                                      RUNNING @ 10:01:01
                                      FAILED  @ 10:01:10

                                    Network reorders them:

                                    FAILED arrives
                                    RUNNING arrives later

                                    You don't want:

                                    FAILED → RUNNING   ❌

                                    So the event should carry something like:

                                    attempt_id
                                    status
                                    sequence_number
                                    timestamp

                                    Then Task Status Service can effectively say:

                                    if sequence_number <= last_processed_sequence:
                                           ignore
                                    else:
                                           apply

                                    Timestamp can work, but monotonic sequence/epoch is safer because clocks aren't reliable for ordering.

                                    3. Permanent state is in DB

                                    Yes. This is the key simplification.

                                    Your model is essentially:

                                                      DB
                                                       |
                                              +--------+--------+
                                              |                 |
                                           Job state         Task state
                                                                  |
                                                             Attempt state

                                    Controllers are stateless/recoverable processors.

                                    If Job Controller dies:

                                    Job Controller A
                                          X
                                          |
                                          v
                                    Job Controller B
                                          |
                                          v
                                          DB
                                          |
                                          v
                                    continue from persisted state

                                    Same for Scheduler.

                                    That's a very standard and good architecture.

                                    4. I actually agree with your sidecar + poller combination

                                    Earlier we were discussing event-driven status, but your clarification makes the model more practical:

                                    Container
                                        |
                                     Sidecar health/status
                                        |
                                        v
                                    Status store
                                        ^
                                        |
                                      Poller
                                        |
                                        v
                                    Task Controller

                                    The important distinction is:

                                    the poller isn't discovering task state from the container.

                                    The sidecar continuously publishes the state, and the poller is reading the durable state.

                                    That gives you recovery.

                                    For example:

                                    10:00  A1 RUNNING
                                    10:01  A1 RUNNING
                                    10:02  A1 RUNNING
                                    10:03  A1 RUNNING

                                    Scheduler crashes at 10:04.

                                    New scheduler comes up:

                                    read DB
                                      |
                                      +-- A1 RUNNING
                                      +-- A2 SUCCEEDED
                                      +-- A3 FAILED

                                    It can simply continue.

                                    5. One subtle point: "permanent" state doesn't mean blindly trust RUNNING

                                    This is the one place I'd be careful.

                                    Suppose DB says:

                                    A1 = RUNNING
                                    last heartbeat = 20 minutes ago

                                    The DB state is durable, but liveness isn't durable.

                                    So the controller needs:

                                    state = RUNNING
                                    +
                                    last_heartbeat = old
                                            |
                                            v
                                    consider LOST
                                            |
                                            v
                                    retry / restart

                                    This is why your sidecar health publisher is useful.

                                    So I'd distinguish:

                                    DB:
                                        What was the last known state?

                                    Heartbeat:
                                        Is this execution still alive?

                                    That's a clean separation.

                                    And your "two attempts" problem now fits nicely

                                    You don't actually have:

                                    Task T1
                                    Task T1

                                    You have:

                                                  TASK T1
                                                     |
                                              +------+------+
                                              |             |
                                           Attempt A1    Attempt A2
                                              |             |
                                           Container      Container
                                              |             |
                                           heartbeat      heartbeat

                                    DB records everything.

                                    If A1 becomes unhealthy:

                                    A1 → LOST
                                         |
                                         +----> A2 created

                                    If A2 succeeds:

                                    TASK T1
                                    status = SUCCEEDED
                                    winner = A2

                                    If A1 later comes back and says:

                                    A1 = SUCCEEDED

                                    that's fine. Attempt A1 succeeded physically; Task T1 had already succeeded logically with A2.

                                    That's probably the cleanest resolution of the race you've been uncovering.

                                    So yes: I'd now call your architecture fairly complete.

                                    At this point, don't add more components. The interesting interview discussion is going to be the semantics:

                                    "What exactly happens when the scheduler, sidecar, status service, or DB fails at each point in this lifecycle?"

                                    That's where you can demonstrate the distributed-systems depth rather than adding architecture boxes.